                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It is nice that the male characters have several sprites (-looks). The artwork is pleasant to look at.\r\nOthers are bashing the choices of activities per day. That alone didn't hurt this experience.\r\n\r\nWhat I disapprove of is the writing. The interesting part of the game story is blocked out, by \"rules\". Then I have read in a guide that you need to concentrate on the orange cat, because he will disappear and won't be able to raise your heart meter with him. I did just that after getting everybody up to ~25 points, keeping the heroine from being stressed or pressured... and I got a bad ending? The bad ending didn't seem all that bad if you ask me, unless the heroine is such an idiot that she doesn't say anything against an unwanted outcome. Even if we ignore that the ending came out of nowhere, abruptly with high relationship points with a \"good\" ending character, you are not even picking dialogue options.\r\nNot being able to participate in the way of the story is being formed, and just watching the heroine stumble into plots, disconnects the player on multiple levels.\r\n\r\nThis is not a visual novel, and a bad raise your daughter simulator in which save scumming is required to catch characters in convo at the right time.\r\n\r\nOh, and inventory management... is non existent. You will inevitably fill your inventory with junk to collect stickers to unlock extras. You have to pick them up, and waste days at the store getting rid of them by selling them for a total of 0 coins.",
